{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1198. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler known for his military skills political acumen and economic policies. His reign was marked by both expansion and internal challenges reflecting the turbulent political landscape of the Levant during the medieval period.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He received extensive military training and demonstrated leadership qualities from a young age. Upon his father's death in 1154 Raymond inherited the County of Tripoli a strategically important territory on the coast of Syria. His early rule was focused on consolidating his power and defending the county against various enemies including Muslim forces and rival Crusader states. He faced challenges from the Zengi dynasty of Mosul a powerful Muslim emirate that sought to expand its influence in the region. Raymond skillfully navigated these conflicts forming alliances and conducting military campaigns to maintain Tripoli's independence.\n\nRaymond III’s reign saw significant territorial gains. He expanded the county's control over several key areas including Tyre and Sidon bolstering Tripoli's economic and military strength. He actively participated in Crusades fighting alongside other European knights against Muslim forces. His military campaigns were often characterized by bold tactics and decisive victories. He was known for his personal bravery and his ability to inspire his troops.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a shrewd diplomat adept at forging alliances and maintaining a delicate balance of power. He cultivated relationships with the kings of Europe particularly with Louis VII of France and Frederick II of Hohenstaufen. These alliances provided Tripoli with crucial military and financial support. He also engaged in diplomatic negotiations with Muslim rulers seeking to establish peaceful relations and trade agreements. His diplomatic efforts aimed to secure Tripoli's long-term stability and prosperity. He understood the importance of economic prosperity and implemented policies to encourage trade and commerce. Tripoli flourished under his rule becoming a major center for the exchange of goods between Europe and the East.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy*\n\nIn his later years Raymond III faced increasing pressure from the growing power of the Zengids and the Seljuk Turks. Despite these challenges he continued to defend Tripoli with determination. He died in 1198 leaving the county to his son Raymond IV. Raymond III's reign is remembered as a golden age for the County of Tripoli a period of prosperity military strength and cultural flourishing. He is regarded as one of the most successful and influential rulers of the Crusader states. His legacy extends beyond military achievements encompassing his diplomatic skills economic policies and contributions to the cultural development of Tripoli. He left behind a well-defended and prosperous county a testament to his leadership and vision.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.24395672976970673}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.310844361782074}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.07919970899820328}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00017081231635529548}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.6702524337451905e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.22540732769994e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.814624506048858e-05},